Migraine
Young Adult & Adolecence
Unilateral throbbing headache, Nausea/vomiting, Photophobia/Phonophobia, Exacerbation by routine activity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing unilateral headache accompanied by intractable vomiting, severe photophobia, and inability to maintain oral hydration or analgesia.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage detailed a patient suffering from a sudden, excruciating unilateral headache, recurrent emesis, and visual disturbances, rendering them completely incapacitated. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to rule out life-threatening intracranial pathology (e.g., subarachnoid hemorrhage or meningitis) and to provide immediate parenteral antiemetics and analgesia to break the severe pain cycle prior to the availability of the regular GP.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The right side of my head is throbbing so violently I can't open my eyes, and I throw up immediately if I try to drink water."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 12-hour history of an acute, severe unilateral pulsating and throbbing headache localized to the right hemicranium. The episode was preceded by a visual aura consisting of scintillating scotomata (flashing zig-zag lines) 30 minutes prior to the pain onset. The headache is accompanied by severe nausea and recurrent vomiting, leading to an inability to tolerate oral medications. The patient exhibits profound photophobia and phonophobia. The head pain is severely exacerbated by routine physical activity, forcing the patient to remain strictly motionless. The patient notes prodromal symptoms of extreme fatigue and mood changes yesterday.
Functional Status: Bedbound; absolutely motionless in a dark, silent room.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is alone, severely dehydrated from vomiting, and completely unable to self-care due to agonizing pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is lying in a fetal position with eyes tightly shut, visibly grimacing, and clutching an emesis basin.
Physical Exam: Comprehensive neurological examination is completely intact; there are no focal motor or sensory deficits, and cranial nerves are normal. Crucially, there is no nuchal rigidity, no positive Kernig's or Brudzinski's signs, and no fever, safely excluding meningeal irritation. Fundoscopy is normal with no papilloedema. Palpation reveals scalp allodynia over the right parietal region. The clinical presentation perfectly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of a unilateral throbbing headache, nausea/vomiting, photophobia/phonophobia, and severe exacerbation by routine activity.
Environmental Observations: All lights are off, blackout blinds are drawn; patient requests the clinician speak in a whisper.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient vomited two doses of over-the-counter Ibuprofen 400mg within 15 minutes of ingestion.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Severe Migraine with Aura and Intractable Emesis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to the presence of a classic preceding visual aura, the gradual build-up over an hour rather than a hyperacute "thunderclap" onset, and completely normal neurological/meningeal exams.

Acute Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strict absence of fever, negative nuchal rigidity, and the patient's history of similar, though less severe, previous episodes.

Tension-Type Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strictly unilateral, throbbing nature of the pain, the presence of a visual aura, and the profound, incapacitating nausea and vomiting.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (Medical risk of dehydration)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute migraine attack complicated by intractable vomiting, rendering standard oral abortive therapies useless. The extreme severity of the pain and the associated tachycardia necessitated an urgent after-hours physical evaluation to safely exclude a catastrophic intracranial event (such as a bleed or infection). Clinical examination confidently excluded meningism and focal neurological deficits, confirming a primary migraine event. Immediate parenteral intervention was strictly necessary to bypass the gastrointestinal tract, arrest the vomiting, and break the severe neurovascular pain cycle, which could not have been delayed until the following morning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat to provide potent parenteral analgesia. Administered Metoclopramide 10mg IM stat to arrest the intractable vomiting and promote gastric emptying. Advised the patient to attempt to sleep immediately following the injections, as sleep frequently facilitates complete resolution of the attack.
Positive Behavioral Change: To actively prevent the transition from a prodrome to a full migraine, take your specific abortive medication (e.g., a triptan) at the very first sign of the visual aura, directly stopping the neurochemical cascade before the severe pain begins. To avoid triggering rebound headaches, strictly limit the use of acute pain medications to fewer than two days per week, consciously preventing the brain from becoming dependent on the analgesia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to discuss prescribing a specific migraine-abortive medication (e.g., Sumatriptan) and to evaluate the need for daily preventative therapy if frequency increases.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the headache suddenly changes to become the "worst headache of your life" instantly, if you develop weakness or numbness on one side of your body, or if you become confused or struggle to speak clearly.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is a primary migraine; if the pattern of headaches changes or neurological symptoms persist, further imaging (like an MRI) may be required.
Clinical Course: The intramuscular injections will significantly reduce the pain and stop the vomiting within 30 to 45 minutes, though you will likely experience a "postdrome" phase of severe fatigue and cognitive clouding tomorrow.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden weakness in your arms or legs, if your speech becomes slurred, or if you develop a high fever and a stiff neck.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A migraine is a complex neurological event causing severe inflammation of the blood vessels around your brain. Because your stomach has shut down and is vomiting, the injected medications bypass the gut to directly block the pain chemicals and stop the nausea pathways in your brain.
Actionable Steps: Try to go to sleep immediately now that the medication is working; deep sleep acts as a hard reset for the brain during a migraine. Tomorrow, drink plenty of water to rehydrate, and rest, as your brain will feel bruised and exhausted.

